menu "BYOK NVS persistence"

    config BYOK_NVS_PERSIST_ARMED
        bool "Persist SET_NOTE text / idle-mode selection / backlight level across reboot (writes the shared nvs partition)"
        default n
        help
            With this OFF (the default), byok_note's stored text, byok_idle's
            selected idle submode (CLOCK/STATIC_NOTE/BLANK) and its selected
            backlight-level index all live in RAM only -- fully usable for
            the running session (SET_NOTE works, the back buttons cycle
            everything), just not remembered across a reboot. No NVS API of
            any kind is called with this off (byok_nvs_init() returns
            immediately, see byok_nvs.h/.c).

            Turning this ON makes byok_nvs_init() call nvs_flash_init() and
            open this firmware's own namespace ("byokmod") inside the
            vendor's shared `nvs` partition (0x9000, firmware/s3/
            partitions.csv) -- the ONLY NVS-type partition this specific
            device's flash actually has; SAFETY.md §1 / partitions.csv's
            own header comment forbid adding a partition-table entry for a
            dedicated store instead. Namespace isolation keeps this
            firmware's keys ("note", "idle_mode", "backlight_lvl", "presets",
            "preset" -- the last two 0.1.14, byok_menu's preset-menu name
            list and selection) strictly
            separate from every vendor namespace (BYOK, RTCI, KBLY, WFST,
            SODU, SYSI, DFNT, CONTR, CTYP, BTKL, BT_K, WIFI, LWSS, DJWT,
            BLBR, DMDE, ACPR -- enumerated from the stock NVS partition) -- NVS
            itself enforces that boundary; nvs_open("byokmod", ...) cannot
            see or touch keys under any other namespace. byok_nvs.c never
            calls nvs_flash_erase() under any condition, on or off.

            docs/design-rationale.md D-003 is an AND-gate (a verified backup AND a
            *tested* recovery path) before any write to this device is
            authorised. The backup half is met for the S3's full flash
            (including `nvs`, byte-for-byte, docs/design-rationale.md's own
            evidence table). The tested-recovery-path half has only been
            demonstrated for the OTA app-partition write path (D-016's
            Stage A rehearsal, whose outcome D-018 records) -- nobody has
            rehearsed writing to, or
            restoring, this device's `nvs` partition, this namespace or
            otherwise. Leave this OFF until a decision record (a future
            D-0xx entry, same shape as D-017 for the SD updater's own OTA
            writes) specifically clears that gate for this feature -- not as
            a build-time convenience, and not on a single release's say-so.

endmenu
